AUROC, excluded from our primary metrics because it is threshold-free (\cref{sec:evaluation}), confirms HS discriminability at BA-selected layers ($\Delta$AUROC +12.1 to +15.1pp; \cref{tab:ba_layer_results}) but cannot diagnose the threshold-dependent layer selection bias.\looseness=-1

\paragraph{Ensemble layer selection.} A three-layer ensemble that averages predicted probabilities from the precision-best, BA-best, and inverse-FPR-best layers bypasses single-metric pre-selection entirely. On the holdout split, all four models show significant HS advantage under the ensemble ($p \leq 0.002$, HB corrected; \cref{tab:ensemble}), including both 32B models that fail under CR-composite selection. The ensemble succeeds because probability averaging across layers spanning the full depth range smooths metric-specific artifacts: even when constituent layers include both shallow CR-favored and deep threshold-favored regions, the averaged prediction reflects genuine discriminative signal rather than compression noise. This holdout result uses the same split for both layer selection and evaluation; cross-validated confirmation is needed before the ensemble can be recommended as a general debiasing strategy (\cref{sec:discussion}).\looseness=-1

